From c11674c95288210d74deb36bd0623b4d90fa02bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 07:07:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ActiveAE: the list of the outputs read from a snapshot that
 an enumeration publishes whole

CActiveAESink::EnumerateSinkList() made the list of the outputs of every driver again in place: it
cleared m_sinkInfoList, then let the drivers fill it (CAESinkFactory::EnumerateEx(), which may take
seconds, and tries again with a sleep of 1.5 s while nothing is found). Other threads read that
vector meanwhile, with no lock: the settings and JSON-RPC (CActiveAE::EnumerateOutputDevices(), and
the settings that ask what an output takes: SupportsRaw(), IsSettingVisible()), the thread of the
sink (OpenSink(), GetDeviceFriendlyName(), NeedIECPacking()), and the output of the display that
Hades looks for when a display comes or goes (CActiveAE::OutputOfTheDisplay()), which is exactly
when the engine enumerates again. A reader could walk a vector being cleared or growing: entries
destroyed or moved under it.

Now the list is a snapshot, std::shared_ptr<const std::vector<AE::AESinkInfo>>, held in a
std::atomic (C++20, as Kodi is built): EnumerateSinkList() makes the next list apart, with no lock
held and with its retries as before, and publishes it whole when it is done; every reader of the
list (fifteen of them) takes the snapshot of the moment once (SinkInfo()) and reads that, with no
lock held, as long as it needs (a reference into it stays valid while the reader holds it). No
reader waits for an enumeration, and none holds a lock while it calls other code. What each reader
returns does not change; the readers read through const iterators and references, and PrintSinks()
prints a copy of each output (its text is made by a member that is not const). The one difference
is the moment between two lists: a reader on another thread goes on with the list before until the
new one is published, where it used to see a list half made or none.

Checked on the host: the readers, EnumerateSinkList() and the helpers of ActiveAESink.cpp as the
tree has them, with the real AESinkFactory.h, AEDeviceInfo.cpp, AEChannelInfo.cpp and
ActiveAEFollowDisplay.h, and drivers made for the test: every reader for twelve devices on two lists,
and again after one driver is enumerated again, gives the same 30 lines of results before and after
the change. An enumeration again and again on one thread and the readers on three others: on the code
before, 157 data races under ThreadSanitizer and a heap-use-after-free under AddressSanitizer
(GetFriendlyName() of an output that the enumeration had freed); with the snapshot, none, in six
passes of 108000 reads each with ThreadSanitizer and with AddressSanitizer.
